e could meaningfully move the margin.", "confidence": "low", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Texas remains a Republican-leaning state at the presidential level, and the party's White House incumbent is term-limited, leaving an open race with no incumbency advantage for either side. With nominees undetermined, there are no general-election polls or expert ratings to draw on, and early attention has centered on the parties' 2028 primaries rather than the Texas matchup.\n\nThe state's partisan fundamentals point to a modest Republican edge of roughly six points. Uncertainty is wide: candidate quality, the national climate, and Democratic decisions about investing in a deep-red state — a question already being debated in party circles — could all move the margin. Until direct polling emerges, the race is best treated as leaning Republican with substantial uncertainty.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.2466, "margin": -6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.2898, "margin": -6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.2886, "margin": -6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.2879, "margin": -6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.2903, "margin": -6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.2829, "margin": -6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.2913, "margin": -6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.2909, "margin": -6.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.2878, "margin": -6.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.2866, "margin": -6.0}, {"date": "2026-10-03", "p_dem": 0.288, "margin": -6.0}, {"date": "2026-10-04", "p_dem": 0.2839, "margin": -6.0}, {"date": "2026-10-05", "p_dem": 0.2914, "margin": -6.0}]}